Root cause:
The tunnel transmit functions `iptunnel_xmit` and `ip6tunnel_xmit` lacked any recursion limit. When a bond device in broadcast mode has GRE tap interfaces as slaves that route back through the bond, multicast/broadcast traffic triggers an infinite recursion between `bond_xmit_broadcast()` and the tunnel xmit path, consuming excessive kernel stack space and eventually causing a stack overflow (KASAN stack-out-of-bounds). The existing general xmit recursion limit (8) in the no-qdisc path is insufficient because each tunnel recursion involves route lookups and full IP output, using much more stack per level.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
`void iptunnel_xmit(struct sock *sk, struct rtable *rt, struct sk_buff *skb, ...)`
`bond_xmit_broadcast(struct sk_buff *skb, struct net_device *bond_dev)`
Reason:
`iptunnel_xmit` lacks any recursion limit, so when a bond device in broadcast mode has GRE tap slaves that route back through the bond, multicast/broadcast traffic causes infinite recursion between `bond_xmit_broadcast` and `iptunnel_xmit`. Each recursion consumes significant stack due to route lookups and IP output, quickly leading to stack overflow (KASAN stack-out-of-bounds). The existing general recursion limit (XMIT_RECURSION_LIMIT=8) in the no-qdisc path is insufficient because tunnel recursion uses more stack per level.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
`if (dev_recursion_level() > IP_TUNNEL_RECURSION_LIMIT) { net_crit_ratelimited(...); DEV_STATS_INC(dev, tx_errors); ip_rt_put(rt); kfree_skb(skb); return; }`
`dev_xmit_recursion_inc();`
`dev_xmit_recursion_dec();`
Reason:
The patch adds a dedicated recursion check using `dev_recursion_level()` at the beginning of `iptunnel_xmit` (and similarly for `ip6tunnel_xmit`). If the recursion level exceeds `IP_TUNNEL_RECURSION_LIMIT` (set to 4), it drops the packet and logs a critical message, preventing stack overflow. After the check, it increments the recursion counter with `dev_xmit_recursion_inc()` before proceeding, and decrements with `dev_xmit_recursion_dec()` after `ip_local_out`, ensuring proper counter tracking. This limits the recursion depth and protects against infinite loops across all IPv4/IPv6 tunnel paths.


